# `(* x (* y *) z *)` - OCaml's comments nest, and unlike Swift's and Scala's
# they are the grammar's *only* comment form, so the row `comment/.marrow/
# .ocaml_comment` is the whole of OCaml's comment handling.
#
# A non-nesting reader closes at the first `*)`, byte 22, and then meets
# `z *)` as code. Only a hand carrying a depth counter reaches [10, 27), and
# the name alone does not separate them - the extent does.
#
# Un-seated (`aud-r5`) the body does not come back as a short comment; `*` is
# an operator in OCaml, so it comes back as `mult_operator` at 11, 16 and 25
# with a `value_path` between them, and the first `let` loses its
# `value_definition`. `lacks mult_operator` is that failure, stated as a claim.
#
# Written 2026-08-06. This was the third of the three rows
# `research/joinery/consort/RESULT-4-clearance.md` found corpus-only, and all
# three had negative measured worth - the corpus board's only opinion of this
# row was that it does harm.
holds comment
spans comment 10 27
lacks mult_operator
